# Code owners.
#
# Ownership here is not about territory. It is about which files, if changed
# carelessly, would weaken the controls this repository exists to demonstrate.
# A change to a gate or to the branch rules is a change to the control
# environment, and a control environment that anyone can edit unreviewed is
# not a control environment.
#
# One honest note, because it matters now that the branch ruleset is applied:
# this repository currently has one maintainer, and GitHub does not let an author
# satisfy a code owner review on their own pull request. Until there is a second
# reviewer, the ruleset's bypass for repository administrators is what keeps the
# maintainer able to merge, and GitHub records every use of it. See
# docs/decisions/0003-private-first-and-what-that-turns-off.md.

# Everything, by default.
*                               @keonikaku

# The pipeline itself.
/.github/workflows/             @keonikaku

# The branch rules, the owners file, and the pull request template. Anything
# that can be used to weaken a gate rather than to pass it.
/.github/rulesets/              @keonikaku
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @keonikaku
/.github/pull_request_template.md @keonikaku
/.github/dependabot.yml         @keonikaku

# The gate configuration. Lowering a coverage threshold or disabling a lint rule
# is a decision about the control set, not a tidy up.
/biome.json                     @keonikaku
/tsconfig.json                  @keonikaku
/vite.config.ts                 @keonikaku
/playwright.config.ts           @keonikaku

# The documents that state what is enforced. A claim in here that the pipeline
# does not support is the failure this repository is most keen to avoid.
/docs/                          @keonikaku
